      <div class="ch-num">Chapter 1 · The Diagnosis</div>
      <h2>Why Conventional Computing Pays a Cartesian Tax</h2>
      <p>Start with an honest question: why does a single software-and-hardware stack today require a 28-million-line Linux kernel, multi-level MESI cache coherence directories, tracing garbage collectors, separate unit-test harnesses, external synthesis heuristics, and prose docs that constantly rot out of sync with code?</p>
      <p>Because conventional engineering models each concern as an independent unverified layer and pays the <strong>Cartesian product</strong> of every boundary between them:</p>
      <div class="eq-box">$$\text{Conventional Complexity} \;\sim\; \mathrm{ISA} \times \mathrm{ROB} \times \mathrm{MESI} \times \mathrm{GC} \times \mathrm{Compiler} \times \mathrm{Tests} \times \mathrm{Docs}$$</div>
      <p>Every unmechanized boundary is a place where truth silently diverges:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Spec vs. Hardware Drift:</strong> A compiler emits pin transactions or RTL as untyped strings while a separate function computes the "expected" answer. If one side changes, the testbench quietly checks the wrong invariant or only catches the bug at runtime.</li>
        <li><strong>Artifact vs. Metric Drift:</strong> A layout generator emits a hierarchical GDSII chip array, while a human hand-multiplies polygon counts for documentation (e.g., an extra $16\times$ factor turning <code>1,166,552</code> polygons into <code>18,664,712</code>).</li>
        <li><strong>Code vs. Citation Drift:</strong> A theorem is renamed or refined in code while READMEs, papers, and web pages keep citing the dead name.</li>
        <li><strong>Execution vs. Trust Drift:</strong> On a network, Peer A sends Peer B a bare output; Peer B must either blindly trust Peer A or re-execute the entire search from scratch.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Hyperactivity eliminates this entire class of failure at the root by unifying <strong>execution</strong> (local degree-2 interaction nets), <strong>meaning</strong> (a zero-postulate cubical dependent type theory), and <strong>storage</strong> (an immutable content-addressed object pool with no mutable filesystem).</p>

