    <section class="chapter" id="ch2">
      <div class="ch-num">Chapter 2 · The Computational Atom</div>
      <h2>Degree-2 Interaction Nets from Zero (No Clock, No Stack, No GC)</h2>
      <p>Forget instructions, registers, and heaps for five minutes. Imagine a machine built from just two ingredients: <strong>cells</strong> and <strong>wires</strong>. Every wire has exact degree 2 (two endpoints — it can never secretly fork or dangle). Every cell has one <strong>principal port</strong> (the dot on its edge) and zero or more <strong>auxiliary ports</strong>, and belongs to one of two polarities:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Value (<code>V_*</code>, pink octagon):</strong> passive data constructors — numbers, bits, booleans, pairs, functions, paths.</li>
        <li><strong>Operation (<code>O_*</code>, blue rectangle):</strong> active consumers — <code>+</code>, <code>×</code>, <code>cmp</code>, <code>app</code>, duplicator <code>δ</code>, eraser <code>ε</code>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Whenever a Value's principal port meets an Operation's principal port head-to-head, they form an <strong>active pair (<code>●═●</code>)</strong>. Firing that pair rewrites <em>only those two cells and their immediate wires</em>. Disjoint active pairs anywhere in the graph fire simultaneously with zero locks, zero cache coherence traffic, and guaranteed confluence (strong Church–Rosser).</p>

      <h3>2.1 Natural Numbers &amp; Addition from Nothing</h3>
      <p>Peano numbers require just two Value cells: <code>Z</code> (zero, 0 aux ports) and <code>S</code> (successor, 1 aux port). Addition (<code>+</code>) needs just two local rewrite rules: <code>+ ⋈ Z ⟶</code> wire <code>y</code> straight out; <code>+ ⋈ S(n) ⟶ S(+ ⋈ n)</code>. Click any glowing pink wire below, press <strong>Play</strong>, or edit <code>add 2 3</code> to <code>add 4 5</code> and click <strong>Compile</strong>:</p>
      <p><a href="/nets?preset=add">Unary Addition: add 2 3 →</a></p>

      <pre><code>data Nat : Set where
  zero : Nat
  suc  : Nat → Nat

add : Nat → Nat → Nat
add zero    y = y
add (suc n) y = suc (add n y)</code></pre>

      <h3>2.2 Explicit Sharing (<code>δ</code>) &amp; Erasure (<code>ε</code>) Kill Garbage Collection</h3>
      <p>Addition uses each input once. Multiplication (<code>0 × y = 0</code>, <code>(n+1) × y = y + n × y</code>) either <em>discards</em> <code>y</code> or uses <code>y</code> <em>twice</em>. Because wires are strictly degree-2, copying and discarding are explicit, local cells: <strong><code>δ</code> (<code>O_DUP</code>)</strong> copies one layer on demand, and <strong><code>ε</code> (<code>O_ERA</code>)</strong> dissolves unused cells one layer at a time — deterministic local memory reclamation with zero stop-the-world GC:</p>
      <p><a href="/nets?preset=mul">Multiplication via Sharing (δ) and Erasure (ε): mul 2 3 →</a></p>

      <h3>2.3 Binary Ripple-Carry &amp; Automatic Parallel Sorting</h3>
      <p>Representing numbers in binary LSB-first (<code>end</code>, <code>0</code>, <code>1</code>) shrinks cells from $O(n)$ to $O(\log n)$, turning carry propagation into a local wave. And in divide-and-conquer sorting, disjoint active pairs fire in the exact same depth step with zero threads or mutexes:</p>
      <p><a href="/nets?preset=badd">Binary Ripple-Carry Addition: badd 5 3 →</a></p>
      <p><a href="/nets?preset=sort">Automatic Parallel Compare-and-Swap Sort: sort [3, 1, 4, 2] →</a></p>
    </section>

