      <div class="ch-num">Chapter 3 · The Core Paradigm Shift</div>
      <h2>Proof IS Computational Content (Mechanized Relations)</h2>
      <p>Most people think formal verification means writing a program first and bolting on a separate theorem prover afterward to stamp a badge on it. That is the wrong mental model. In Hyperactivity, <strong>a proof is not a post-hoc badge — a proof is live computational content</strong> that drives compilation, transport, specialization, and zero-trust exchange.</p>
      <p>First, by Curry–Howard, a proposition like <code>add 2 3 ≡ 5</code> is a type, and its proof is an interaction net that reduces to <code>refl</code> — while a false claim like <code>add 2 2 ≡ 5</code> actively reduces to a structural refutation <code>⊥ (≠)</code>. Try editing <code>prove 2+3=5</code> to <code>prove 2+2=5</code> below:</p>
      <p><a href="/nets?preset=prove">Proof Content Computing: prove 2+3=5 →</a></p>
      <p>Second, on a decentralized network, shipping a dependent pair <code>(output , proof-witness) : Σ (xs : List Nat) (Sorted xs × (bag xs ≡ bag input))</code> lets any peer verify correctness locally without trusting the sender or repeating the search:</p>
      <p><a href="/nets?preset=certsort">Proof-Carrying Sort on the Network: certsort [3, 1, 4, 2] →</a></p>

      <h3>3.1 Single-Source Mechanized Relations: Why Bugs Become Unwritable</h3>
      <p>Here is the engineering bar enforced across Hyperactivity: <strong>every relied-on property must be proven inline as a mechanized relation, hyperfactored so proof length never exceeds the size of the rationale</strong>. Instead of maintaining two disconnected representations (e.g., an emitter and a spec, or a GDSII byte stream and a polygon count), you define <em>one</em> algebraic AST and project both the artifact and the semantics from it:</p>
